   "text": "Read only the page below. Read no other file, and do not run commands or browse. Answer each question from the page alone. When the page does not say, answer \"Not stated\".\n\n<<<PAGE\n# Know what held a local TCP port\n\nportlog records which process held each local TCP port over time, so you can look back after the process exits.\n\n```sh\nbrew install example/tap/portlog\n```\n\nmacOS and Linux · Written in Go · MIT license\n\n## What was on port 3000 an hour ago?\n\n```sh\nportlog who 3000 --since 2h\n```\n\nShow every process recorded on port 3000 in the last two hours, newest first.\n\n## Commands\n\n| Command | What it does |\n| --- | --- |\n| `portlog start` | Start the background recorder. |\n| `portlog stop` | Stop the recorder. |\n| `portlog who 3000` | Show every process that held port 3000, newest first. |\n| `portlog free 3000` | Stop the process holding port 3000 now, after confirmation. |\n\n## Keys\n\n| Option | Effect |\n| --- | --- |\n| `--since 2h` | Limit a `who` query to the last two hours. |\n| `-y` | Skip confirmation for `free`. |\n\n## Good to know\n\n**History**  \nRecordings stay in `~/.portlog/` for 7 days.\n\n**Command-line arguments**  \nportlog never records command-line arguments, because they can hold secrets.\n\n**Recording method**  \nOn macOS, the recorder polls `lsof` every 2 seconds. On Linux, it reads `/proc/net/tcp`.\n\n[Full reference](README.md)\nPAGE>>>\n\nQuestions:\n(a) What does portlog do?\n(b) What do you run first after installing it?\n(c) Can it show a process that already exited?\n\nReply with only JSON: {\"a\": \"...\", \"b\": \"...\", \"c\": \"...\"}. Start the answer to (c) with Yes, No, or Not stated.",
